      <section class="sg">
        <h2>What Australian trade businesses sell for in 2026</h2>
        <p class="sg-intro">Buyers value a trade business on a multiple of EBITDA — your adjusted net profit before interest, tax, depreciation, amortisation and non-recurring items. The multiple is set by how much of that profit survives you leaving.</p>
        <div class="sg-block">
          <h3>Typical EBITDA multiples, electrical &amp; trade businesses</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Owner-operator, no systems, no recurring revenue</strong><span class="sg-amt">1.0–1.5× &nbsp;·&nbsp; $200K EBITDA ≈ $200K–$300K</span></li>
            <li><strong>Small team, some systems, limited recurring revenue</strong><span class="sg-amt">1.5–2.5× &nbsp;·&nbsp; $200K EBITDA ≈ $300K–$500K</span></li>
            <li><strong>Established team, documented systems, some contracts</strong><span class="sg-amt">2.5–3.5× &nbsp;·&nbsp; $200K EBITDA ≈ $500K–$700K</span></li>
            <li><strong>Strong recurring revenue, owner-independent, diversified</strong><span class="sg-amt">3.5–5.0× &nbsp;·&nbsp; $200K EBITDA ≈ $700K–$1M</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-block">
          <h3>The 8 value drivers the calculator scores</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Recurring revenue</strong> — maintenance contracts and repeat clients a buyer can bank on</li>
            <li><strong>Owner independence</strong> — can the business run a month without you?</li>
            <li><strong>Documented systems</strong> — quoting, scheduling and job processes written down</li>
            <li><strong>Clean books</strong> — reconciled accounts and add-backs a buyer's accountant will accept</li>
            <li><strong>Compliance history</strong> — certificates, licences and safety records that survive due diligence</li>
            <li><strong>Team depth</strong> — licensed staff who stay after settlement</li>
            <li><strong>Customer concentration</strong> — no single client carrying the revenue</li>
            <li><strong>Transferability</strong> — contracts, supplier terms and IP that move with the sale</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="sg sg-faq">
        <h2>Trade business valuation: common questions</h2>
        <details>
          <summary>How much is my electrical business worth in Australia?</summary>
          <p>Most small-to-medium electrical businesses sell for 1.0–5.0× EBITDA depending on systems, recurring revenue and owner dependence. An owner-operator with no systems typically sees 1.0–1.5×; a systematised, owner-independent business with contracted revenue can reach 3.5–5.0×.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What lifts a trade business multiple fastest?</summary>
          <p>Recurring maintenance revenue and owner independence move the multiple more than top-line growth. Documented systems and clean, reconciled books are what let a buyer's accountant accept the profit figure you are claiming — without them the multiple compresses toward the owner-operator band.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is this valuation free, and do I need to give my email?</summary>
          <p>The calculator is free and gives you a price range and sellability scorecard in about 90 seconds. You only enter an email if you want the report sent to you.</p>
        </details>
